{#
    A subscription's logo, or the Renovo mark when it has none.

    The same three lines used to be pasted into four templates, and this round
    added three more places that wanted them. One macro instead, so the
    fallback, the size and the accessibility treatment are decided once — the
    failure this replaces is a screen that quietly renders no logo at all
    because whoever added it copied the `<img>` and not the `{% if %}`.

    **The fallback costs nothing to fetch.** It is a `<use>` of the SVG symbol
    `partials/brand_sprite.twig` already emits once per signed-in page, so a
    table of thirty subscriptions with no logos makes no requests and carries no
    second copy of the path data. It also means the fallback follows the theme:
    the symbol is filled with the brand gradient by way of the tokens.

    Both forms are decorative and hidden from assistive technology. The
    subscription's name is always next to the mark and is the thing that
    identifies it; announcing "Renovo" beside every unbranded row would be
    noise, and announcing the name twice is worse.

    `subscription`  anything with `logoPath` and `name`
    `size`          the box to draw it in, in pixels. 24 suits a table row.

    The mark's own aspect ratio is 460:427, so it is drawn into a square box
    rather than stretched to fill one — the default `preserveAspectRatio`
    letterboxes it, which is why no width/height juggling is needed here.
#}
{% macro mark(subscription, size = 24) %}
    {%- if subscription.logoPath -%}
        <img class="logo" src="/{{ subscription.logoPath }}" alt=""
             width="{{ size }}" height="{{ size }}">
    {%- else -%}
        <svg class="logo logo-fallback" width="{{ size }}" height="{{ size }}"
             viewBox="0 0 460 427" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <use href="#renovo-mark"></use>
        </svg>
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
